rminers-quantifiers-50","fingerprint":"6bcdea8d080b970e0120eb5c21a5632d050885b5b45eb20c002b397b17573e8d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-determiners"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Both means **the two together**.","**Both** selects two things and takes plural agreement.","Choose **are**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Both** selects two things and takes plural agreement.","B":"Is is singular; both refers to two.","C":"Am agrees only with I.","D":"Be is not a finite present form here."}}},{"id":"a94a3c3c-208a-54ba-a5aa-7296e3d1fe97","topicSlug":"determiners-quantifiers","level":"A2","prompt":"“Most ____ the cups in the picture are labelled.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"than"},{"id":"B","text":"of"},{"id":"C","text":"—"},{"id":"D","text":"to"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"There is already a determiner **the**, so use **most of the**.","explanation":"Before **the + noun**, most uses **of**.","keywords":["most-of-determined"],"skill":"most-of-determined","contextKey":"determiners-quantifiers-50","fingerprint":"4d82fc1225c2d86ffe2043ad0527d667e873e666340224e0db1a9f6ea56bbdc6","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-determiners"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["There is already a determiner **the**, so use **most of the**.","Before **the + noun**, most uses **of**.","Choose **of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Than is used in comparisons, not after most in this quantity phrase.","B":"Before **the + noun**, most uses **of**.","C":"Most the ... omits the required of.","D":"To does not form this partitive pattern."}}},{"id":"b1ffed9a-3dc1-5855-a4e1-a6f24a090fed","topicSlug":"determiners-quantifiers","level":"A2","prompt":"Exactly two cups were considered, and neither was selected. What does “neither” mean here?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Not one and not the other."},{"id":"B","text":"Both were selected."},{"id":"C","text":"One was selected, but we do not know which."},{"id":"D","text":"All but one were selected."}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The context names **two choices** and excludes each of them.","explanation":"**Neither** excludes both members of the stated pair.","keywords":["neither-meaning"],"skill":"neither-meaning","contextKey":"determiners-quantifiers-50","fingerprint":"c839cb630c8473df9887635c0d1082fe428b004cbfa58009fcd648736ede155e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-determiners"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The context names **two choices** and excludes each of them.","**Neither** excludes both members of the stated pair.","Choose **Not one and not the other.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**Neither** excludes both members of the stated pair.","B":"That is the opposite of neither.","C":"That would leave one selected; neither excludes both.","D":"With two items, that would select one, contradicting neither."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Choose quantity and reference words from number, meaning and the type of noun phrase.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Some and any","explanation":"Some is common in positive statements; any is common in negatives and open questions. Some is also natural in offers and requests when a positive response is anticipated. Any can mean it does not matter which in positive clauses.","pattern":"Would you like **some** tea? / Choose **any** seat.","commonMistakes":["Some and any are not fixed positive/negative opposites in every context."],"examples":["“Every book in this picture **has** a label.”","“Every box in this picture **has** a label.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Every and each","explanation":"Every and each usually take a singular count noun and singular agreement. Every views the members as a complete group; each foregrounds them individually. Each of takes a plural noun phrase but the head each remains singular.","pattern":"**Every student has** a book. / **Each of the students has** one.","commonMistakes":["Every students mixes a singular determiner pattern with a plural noun."],"examples":["“Both books shown here **are** clearly labelled.”","“Both boxes shown here **are** clearly labelled.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Both, either and neither","explanation":"Both refers to two together. Either selects one of two or leaves either choice open. Neither excludes both. Before a noun, either and neither usually take a singular count form; both takes plural. Usage after neither of can vary, especially informally.","pattern":"**Both seats are** free. / **Neither seat is** free.","commonMistakes":["Do not add not to neither merely to express the same negative meaning."],"examples":["“Most **of** the books in the picture are labelled.”","“Most **of** the boxes in the picture are labelled.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"All, most and of","explanation":"Use all or most directly before a general plural noun. Before a determiner or object pronoun, use an of-pattern where required: most of the books, all of them. All can often omit of before the: all the books.","pattern":"**Most books** / **Most of the books** / **All of them**","commonMistakes":["Most the books is missing of. Avoid assuming all and most have identical rules in every pattern."],"examples":["Exactly two books were considered, and neither was selected. What does “neither” mean here? → **Not one and not the other.**","Exactly two boxes were considered, and neither was selected. What does “neither” mean here? → **Not one and not the other.**"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["Would you like **some** tea? / Choose **any** seat.","**Every student has** a book. / **Each of the students has** one.","**Both seats are** free. / **Neither seat is** free.","**Most books** / **Most of the books** / **All of them**"]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["small-quantities"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
